<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 45 M3, AC3.7 / T17: A COUNT-IN AND A RECORD START UNDER A CLOSURE.

  record_count_in_primed with ONE thing added: a 2.0x insert on the master, put
  there BEFORE the record start. That makes the master shape non-linear and
  LANE-caused, so the live lane the count-in raises is a CLOSURE rather than a
  linear split, and all three claims below are then about the Closure path.

  WHY THIS CASE EXISTS AT ALL. AC3.1's engine half re-roots the frozen lane
  below the master chain while a Closure is live, and the count-in's priming
  demand (twSpeaker::warmFrozenLane) is issued through a DIFFERENT accessor
  from the readahead's, so it needed its own re-rooting. The first write-up of
  that change called this combination unreachable; it is not. Monitor AUTO is
  "input while stopped OR recording", so a Closure is live with the transport
  STOPPED, and a record start with a count-in demands priming pages from
  exactly there. Rooted ABOVE the master those freezes would run the master
  lane's processors on a worker while the pump owns them.

  THREE CLAIMS, and each one would survive the other two being wrong:

    assert-priming          the count-in still PRIMES with a master insert up.
                            The re-rooting must not have cost the warm-up its
                            effect: rooted at the bus sum it still covers the
                            window startPlayback waits for, because that wait
                            reads a frontier the readahead maintains against
                            whatever root it demands.
    assert-recorded-clip    the take lands at the SAME frame as with a linear
                            master. A recording captures the INPUT device, so
                            the master chain cannot reach it, and the placement
                            conversion must be indifferent to the mode.
    assert-render-policy    no freeze worker met a live-owned master processor
                            during the count-in.

  THE MASTER INSERT CANNOT REACH THE TAKE, which is why 2.0x is safe here and
  why no level assertion appears: SAudioRecorder writes what the input device
  delivered, never what the output produced.

  Everything else, including the nine-track cold fixture and the 20 s pitched
  material, is record_count_in_primed's and its header is the reasoning for it.
  maxPolls stays at 1 for the same reason it does there: if it flakes, that is
  the number to look at LAST, after the warm-up and the re-rooting.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_count_in_master_insert">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-tempo bpm="120"/>

    <!-- The material: pitched, so a page costs the vocoder real work, and at
         20 s so it is nowhere near what a revalidation from 0 would cover. -->
    <add-sample trackPath="1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="1,0" cents="700"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="2" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="2,0" cents="700"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="3" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="3,0" cents="700"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="4" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="4,0" cents="700"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="5" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="5,0" cents="700"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="6" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="6,0" cents="700"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="7" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="7,0" cents="700"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="8" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="960000"/>
    <set-pitch clip="8,0" cents="700"/>

    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:file:1"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="off"/>

    <!-- This case OWNS transport/countInBars and transport/preRollBars. -->
    <set-count-in bars="2"/>
    <set-pre-roll bars="0"/>

    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>
    <set-locator position="960000"/>

    <!-- Let whatever the construction scheduled settle, THEN stale it all. -->
    <wait-ms ms="2000"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="1" volume="-1.0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="2" volume="-1.0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="3" volume="-1.0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="4" volume="-1.0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="5" volume="-1.0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="6" volume="-1.0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="7" volume="-1.0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="8" volume="-1.0"/>

    <!-- THE MASTER INSERT, before the record start, so the count-in's own live
         lane is a CLOSURE from the moment it comes up. -->
    <insert-plugin trackPath="$master" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.gain"
                   name="Smaragd Test Gain" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="2" nOut="2"/>
    <set-plugin-param trackPath="$master" slotIndex="0" paramId="0" value="2.0"/>

    <record-start/>

    <!-- THE CLAIM IS ASSERTED AS A PAIR, and the two halves answer to
         different things on purpose.

         FIRST, THE MECHANISM, and it is LOAD-INDEPENDENT. warmFrozenLane()
         logs the demand it issues, so this line is present iff the count-in
         actually spent itself freezing the priming window. Nothing about a
         busy box can make it absent, and nothing about a fast one can make it
         appear: it is the assertion that would bite if a change removed the
         warm-up or moved it after the click.

         THE TWO HALVES ARE DISJOINT, watched: disabling the warmFrozenLane()
         call fails BOTH, while warming the WRONG POSITION - the call still
         made, the pages still demanded, just not the ones the transport is
         about to want - fails only the priming half. So neither is a
         restatement of the other.
         AC3.1's own hazard - that this demand must be re-rooted UNDER the
         master while the lane is live-owned - is gated separately and equally
         load-independently, by the assert-render-policy line at the end. -->
    <assert-log level="debug" contains="warmFrozenLane: demanded" minCount="1"/>
    <wait-ms ms="4700"/>
    <wait-ms ms="1500"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- THE EFFECT, and the second half of the pair the mechanism
         assertion above opens: the transport barely waited for the readahead,
         because the count-in had already frozen what it needed. THIS ONE IS A
         WALL-CLOCK QUANTITY and is bounded as such, in the same class as
         twlog_test, qxa.log_dock_scale and playback_test: it measures the box
         as much as the code.

         MEASURED ON A 4-CORE BOX, this exact case:

           idle                     0 or 1 polls, 6 runs of 6
           under six spinners       0, 0, 0, 0, 6, 10
           WITH THE WARM-UP OFF     55, 57, 57

         So 12 is chosen to clear the measured loaded tail while keeping a
         factor of more than four below the un-warmed floor. The un-warmed
         number was measured HERE, on this case and this box, by disabling the
         warmFrozenLane() call, rather than quoted from the case next door. It is a WEAKER
         statement than record_count_in_primed's ONE, and deliberately so -
         this case is strictly heavier than that one, because the master
         insert makes the plan a CLOSURE and the pump therefore renders the
         whole nine-track arrangement THROUGH the master lane while the
         count-in primes. The tight number stays next door, on the case that
         can carry it.

         IF THIS FAILS, CHECK THAT THE BOX WAS IDLE FIRST. RUN_SERIAL excludes
         other tests in the same ctest invocation, not a second worktree's
         build or another agent's suite. A reading in the twenties is a
         regression; a reading in the low teens is a loaded machine. And the
         thing to relax is never the warm-up. -->
    <assert-priming maxPolls="12"/>

    <!-- ...and the count-in still places the take AT THE LOCATOR, which is
         what record_count_in gates and what the warm-up must not disturb. -->
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" clips="1" passes="1"
                          startFrame="960000" startTolerance="2048"/>

    <set-count-in bars="0"/>
    <assert-render-policy liveThreadRefusals="0" liveOwnedRefusals="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="9"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
